 **Honest limits (the author's lab, a simulated broker, a TLS bridge to a simulated VPS):** the backend waits 3 seconds for the broker's answer, tries once more, then answers `503`; the database doesn't change (the claim code isn't used up). Two different situations are behind it: (1) the bridge is reconnecting (backoff): a command published at that moment is lost (not held, like `cmd`), and the second attempt usually already succeeds; (2) the home internet drops briefly without the bridge connection breaking (for example less than ±90 seconds): the command is held in that connection and **is still carried out by the gateway broker as soon as the internet is back**, even though the dashboard already answered `503`. So after a `503`, repeat the same step as soon as the bridge is connected again: registering, rotating a key, claiming, and Deactivate are safe to repeat and bring the database and the broker back in line. A repeated Deactivate may be answered `broker_account: "none"` (the account was already deleted by the first command): that's normal. A broker that disconnects a device through dynamic security does **not** publish the `offline` will: the `offline` status is written by the backend itself.
 
+## The Pi updates itself (Module 29)
+
+Since [Module 29](https://github.com/antonprafanto/Full-Stack-IoT-Developer/blob/main/en/phase-5-operations-scale/module-29-testing-cicd/README.md), you no longer SSH into the Pi for `git pull` and `docker compose restart mosquitto`. The systemd alarm clock on the Pi runs [`scripts/deploy.sh`](../scripts/deploy.sh) 5 minutes after the Pi starts, then every 15 minutes (plus a random 0–60 seconds). That script only deploys a **tagged release**: a Git tag `v<version>` on the `main` branch on GitHub, made by the GitHub Actions robot after the tests and staging passed, never an untested `main` commit, and never a tag on another branch. So don't make, move, or delete a `v…` tag yourself (not even through **Releases**): release tags are only made by the robot. The gateway has no image of our own: what gets updated is the official Mosquitto image with an exact tag in `compose.edge.yml` and the broker configuration from the repo.
+
+Every round with `DEPLOY_PARTS=gateway` (the full order is in the script's header):
+
+1. Stop when the repo copy on the Pi isn't on the `main` branch, or when a file was edited directly on the Pi (`git status`).
+2. `git fetch --tags`, then find the latest release tag on `main` on GitHub. Already deployed? Done: `v2.0.1 already deployed (gateway): no new release`.
+3. `git merge --ff-only` to that tag (only straight forward), then from the `edge` folder: `docker compose pull` and `docker compose up -d --no-build --wait`.
+4. `docker compose restart mosquitto` **only** when `gateway.conf` or `acl-broker` changed since the release deployed before, and only once per release (a round that tries again after a failure doesn't restart again). A restart, not `kill -HUP 1`: a HUP throws away the store-and-forward queue (a Module 27 finding), a restart keeps it. Node 1 disconnects for a moment and then reconnects by itself.
+5. Wait 15 seconds, make sure the broker doesn't keep crashing (`Restarting`), then record `v2.0.0 -> v2.0.1 deployed (gateway)`. A broker that only crashes after running for ±10 seconds can slip past this check; a monitor that keeps watching follows in Module 30.
+
+**Installing its alarm clock** (once, on the Pi, from the project root `~/mini-smart-home`; the Pi user must be in the `docker` group, step 5 of the recipe above):
+
+1. `git pull` once by hand, so that `scripts/deploy.sh` and the `scripts/systemd/` folder are on the Pi. After this, no more manual `git pull`: leave it to the script. If that manual pull changes `gateway.conf` or `acl-broker` (from a release after 2.0.0 on), run `docker compose restart mosquitto` once from the `edge` folder: the script's first round doesn't know what changed before it.
+2. Copy both unit files, then adjust the **copies** (an edited file in the repo makes `deploy.sh` stop): `User=` and the path `/home/budi/mini-smart-home` (two places) when your Pi user isn't `budi`, and `DEPLOY_PARTS` when this Pi also runs the center (the free path: `Environment="DEPLOY_PARTS=center gateway"`, with the quotes because of the space). `systemd-analyze verify` for BOTH files without output = no typo (read its output, not only its exit code: a typo in the timer isn't caught when only the `.service` is checked).
+
+   ```bash
+   sudo cp scripts/systemd/smarthome-update.service scripts/systemd/smarthome-update.timer /etc/systemd/system/
+   sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/smarthome-update.service
+   systemd-analyze verify /etc/systemd/system/smarthome-update.service /etc/systemd/system/smarthome-update.timer
+   ```
+
+3. Turn its schedule on, then try one round right now (the last command waits until that round is done):
+
+   ```bash
+   sudo systemctl daemon-reload
+   sudo systemctl enable --now smarthome-update.timer
+   sudo systemctl start smarthome-update.service
+   ```
+
+4. Check: `systemctl list-timers smarthome-update.timer` (when the next round is) and `journalctl -u smarthome-update.service -n 30` (the log of every round).
+
+| In the journal | What it means |
+| :--- | :--- |
+| `no release (tag v<version>) on GitHub yet: nothing deployed` | the robot hasn't made a release tag yet; normal before the pull request that brings version 2.0.0 is merged |
+| `FAIL: files were edited directly on this machine` | a repo file was changed on the Pi. Look at `git diff`, move the change to a pull request from the laptop, then restore the file on the Pi with `git restore <file>` |
+| `FAIL: the repo copy on this machine holds version …` | a manual `git pull` fetched a version that isn't released yet. Nothing is deployed; the next round deploys it as soon as the robot makes its tag |
+| `FAIL: the gateway broker keeps crashing (Restarting)` | the new configuration keeps the broker from starting: `docker compose logs mosquitto` (the `edge` folder), then fix it through a pull request with the next version |
+| `FAIL: don't run this with sudo or as root` | the script was run with `sudo`. Run it as the Pi user (a member of the `docker` group), or leave it to the alarm clock |
+
+**What stays manual:** `edge/.env` and `bridge/center.conf` hold secrets and aren't in Git. If `bridge.example.conf` gets a new line in a new release, copy that line into `bridge/center.conf`, then `docker compose up -d --force-recreate mosquitto` (the `edge` folder), as in Modules 27–28. Throwing away the old images that pile up comes in Module 30. To pause the alarm clock (for example while fixing something by hand): `sudo systemctl disable --now smarthome-update.timer`; to turn it on again: `sudo systemctl enable --now smarthome-update.timer`.
+
+**Honest limits (the author's lab for the Indonesian edition):** the `DEPLOY_PARTS=gateway` rounds were run with real Docker on a simulated gateway (four releases; a restart only when `acl-broker` changed; the dynamic security accounts kept), but not by systemd: release 2.0.0 was run by hand, the next three were woken by a simulated scheduler every ±1 minute. The timer schedule and the waiting `systemctl start` follow the systemd documentation and were checked with `systemd-analyze verify` (systemd 259). The bridge to the center and the simulated Node 1 reconnected by themselves after the restart, within seconds (the Module 29 final lab). This edition's lab (6 November 2026) ran the unit's `ExecStart` by hand on a Compose project standing in for the gateway: 2.0.0, 2.0.1, and 2.0.2 deployed, a round without a new release made no Docker call, and the gateway broker wasn't restarted (those releases didn't change `edge/`).
+
 ## Node-RED and the dashboard (Module 13)
 
 Node-RED runs on this same gateway, as the systemd service `nodered` ([Module 13](https://github.com/antonprafanto/Full-Stack-IoT-Developer/blob/main/en/phase-2-connectivity/module-13-node-red-dashboard/README.md)). All its flows, that is, dashboard v0 (with the Garden card since Module 14 and the RS-485 Sensor group since Module 15) and the fan automation (switched off since Module 16, because Mini Smart Home's brain has moved to the backend), are saved in [`node-red-flows.json`](node-red-flows.json). The editor password and the passphrase are never exported; never write them down here.
